{
  "text": "America paid about two cents an acre for Alaska, and you still can't drive there without a border check. In 1867, the land in between was British. Now it's Canada, still in the way. And America's not the only country in two pieces. In 1963, Malaysia joined a peninsula to northern Borneo. Visiting the other half? That's almost a two-hour flight. Brunei? In 1890, Sarawak took Limbang right out of the middle. The fix 130 years later? A 19-mile bridge around the neighbor. Oman's Musandam guards the Strait of Hormuz with the UAE in between. So by road, to reach its own front door, Oman has to leave the country. So it's always the same trick, someone else's land or a whole sea parked in the middle. In 1885, Portugal signed for Cabinda, and Congo's road to the sea landed in between. Today, Cabinda is Angola on the wrong side of Congo. But the wildest? In 1921, the Treaties of Moscow and Kars gave Nakhchivan to Azerbaijan across Armenia. It borders Iran, Armenia, even a sliver of Turkey, everyone except Azerbaijan. And the cheapest split ever? That's the one where",
